  "query_preview": "..A s/wv ridge and relatively high hghts will bring \nwarm and stable conditions to the region today, after a cool start \nto the morning. This is in advance of a low pressure system that \nwill enter the SOCAL area early Monday morning, ejecting through \nsouthern Nevada and into Utah by Tuesday. This low will draw \nMonsoonal moisture back northward, allowing for showers and \nthunderstorms to redevelop over Arizona.\n\nThis moisture may lead to shower development as nearly as Monday \nafternoon, but is more likely to just result in increased CU/TCU \ndevelopment, rather than QPF. The main chances will arrive Tuesday \nas the trough axis moves through northern Arizona. This will not be \na full blown convective pattern and the moisture will not be as deep \nas last week, but a few stronger cells may produce up to a half inch \nof rainfall.\n\nFor the remainder of the week, persistent southerly flow will keep \nmoisture and shower chances going, but mainly for eastern Arizona as \nthe western zones continue to fight the intrusion of drier air.\n\nLate in the period, models suggest that a pair of sub-tropical \nsystems could inject more significant moisture into our area. \nHowever, confidence is low at this time.",
